Personal Reasoning Corpus (PRC)

A Builder’s Manual

Thesis

Every person already possesses a reasoning corpus.

Most of it is implicit.

The goal is not to invent one.

The goal is to make it explicit, executable, portable, and continuously improvable.

⸻

The Five Primitive Questions

Every artifact should answer only five questions.

1. What did I observe?
2. What changed?
3. What remained invariant?
4. Where else does this operator work?
5. How would I test whether I'm wrong?

Those five questions force knowledge to become reusable.

⸻

The PRC Object

Everything becomes one object.

artifact:
  title:
  observation:
  operator:
  invariant:
  examples:
  counterexamples:
  reconstruction:
  alternate_renderings:
  confidence:
  provenance:
  next_experiments:

Notice there are no essays.

Only executable pieces.

This aligns with your existing corpus pattern of preserving operators, invariants, alternate renderings, provenance, and reconstruction instructions as first-class fields.

